\subsection{GSM8K: sensitivity to answer extraction}
We re-scored the saved beams of two archived runs on the same 50 GSM8K word problems~\cite{cobbe2021gsm8k}. We fixed the extractor before this comparison: it prefers explicitly declared answers, checks the exact numeric value and rejects malformed or conflicting declarations rather than guessing a number from the explanation. All 50 puzzles remain in each denominator. The historical labels gave [[g_old0]]/50 for all-cloud ToT and [[g_old1]]/50 for the split. On the same outputs our rule gave [[g_new0]]/50 and [[g_new1]]/50, respectively (split minus all-cloud [[gdiff]] points, paired 95\% interval $[[gci]]$). Among the changed judgments the old rule truncated thousands-separated answers; the stricter rule also rejected one response with an empty declaration despite a later correct answer. The split used [[gt1]] tokens across both models versus [[gt0]] for the all-cloud run. Fewer tokens did not establish a quality advantage.

\subsection{Limitations}
The archived comparisons use fixed problem lists rather than a blinded holdout. Search configurations lack independent repeated runs. Historical GSM8K prompt examples overlapped some evaluated questions. Intervals resample paired puzzles, not model seeds, and the evaluator-size pilot uses early, relatively easy validation items. Yao et al.~\cite[App.~B.2]{yao2023tree} found that reversing the generator and evaluator roles mattered on Game of 24. We do not replicate that role swap at matched cost, or measure end-to-end local cost and latency. We therefore make no noninferiority, total-cost or speedup claim.
